.TH MITLIST 1 "November 2026" "mitlist 0.1.4" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mitlist \- list MIT Kerberos principals by principal type
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mitlist
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ pattern ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mitlist
is a read-only command-line utility that lists principal names from an MIT Kerberos V5 realm through the remote administration service.
It uses the default Kerberos credential cache and the realm's configured
.B admin_server
from the active Kerberos configuration.
It obtains the required admin-service ticket automatically when needed.
.PP
Principals are grouped into Users, HTTP, CIFS, Host, IMAP, SMTP, LDAP, NFS, and Other categories.
Entries are sorted alphabetically within each category.
Matching of option names, type names, and filters is case-insensitive.
.SH KDC AUTHORIZATION REQUIREMENT
The ordinary Kerberos principal running
.B mitlist
MUST be granted the lowercase
.B l
(list) permission in the KDC's
.B kadm5.acl
file.
For example:
.PP
.nf
alice@EXAMPLE.COM    l
.fi
.PP
The ACL is configured on the KDC, not on the client.
After changing the ACL, the Kerberos administrator must restart
.BR kadmind .
The
.B l
permission allows realm-wide listing of principal and policy names.
It does not grant add, modify, delete, password-change, individual-principal inquiry, or key-extraction permissions.
The listing permission cannot be restricted to a subset of principals by an ACL pattern.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -u ", " --users
List user principals only. Equivalent to
.BR --type users .
.TP
.BR -t " " TYPE ", " --type=TYPE
List only one category. Supported values are
.B users
(or
.BR user ),
.BR HTTP ,
.BR cifs ,
.BR host ,
.BR imap ,
.BR smtp ,
.BR ldap ,
and
.BR nfs .
Type matching is case-insensitive.
.TP
.BR -f " " PATTERN ", " --filter=PATTERN
List only principals matching PATTERN. Matching is case-insensitive and uses shell-style filename pattern syntax as implemented by
.BR fnmatch (3).
The pattern is matched against the complete principal string.
.TP
.BR -r " " REALM ", " --realm=REALM
Only display principals belonging to REALM. The realm comparison is case-insensitive.
This option filters the returned list; the admin server and default credentials are still selected from the active Kerberos configuration and credential cache.
.TP
.BR -i ", " --info
Display the default realm and KDC/admin-server entries explicitly configured for that realm, then exit.
If no KDC is explicitly listed, MIT Kerberos may discover KDCs through DNS according to
.B dns_lookup_kdc
and DNS SRV records. This command reports configuration entries; it does not perform a live KDC discovery or connectivity test.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Display the version and exit.
.TP
.I pattern
An optional positional filter, equivalent to
.BR --filter .
It cannot be specified together with
.BR --filter .
.SH KERBEROS CONFIGURATION
The default realm is determined by MIT Kerberos using its normal configuration and environment rules.
The admin server is read from the realm's
.B admin_server
setting in the active
.BR krb5.conf .
The
.B --info
option shows the default realm and any explicitly configured
.B kdc
and
.B admin_server
values.
Multiple KDC entries can be configured for a realm.
If explicit KDC entries are absent, MIT Kerberos can use DNS-based KDC discovery when enabled.
.PP
A valid TGT must already be present in the default credential cache.
.B mitlist
does not prompt for or store a password and does not require an administrative principal such as
.BR alice/admin@EXAMPLE.COM .
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
List all principals:
.B mitlist
.TP
List users only:
.B mitlist --users
.TP
List HTTP service principals:
.B mitlist --type HTTP
.TP
Filter principals:
.B mitlist --filter 'www*'
.TP
Restrict displayed results to a realm:
.B mitlist --realm EXAMPLE.COM
.TP
Show Kerberos realm and server configuration:
.B mitlist --info
.TP
Show version:
.B mitlist --version
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An error occurred, including invalid options, unavailable Kerberos configuration, failure to obtain the admin-service ticket, or failure of the remote listing operation.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/krb5.conf
Common system-wide MIT Kerberos configuration file. The active configuration may be overridden using
.BR KRB5_CONFIG .
.TP
.I kadm5.acl
KDC-side Kerberos administration ACL file, normally located under the KDC state directory and selected by
.B acl_file
in
.BR kdc.conf .
.SH NOTES
.B mitlist
uses the MIT Kerberos remote administration protocol and the
.B kadmin
client.
It does not read the KDC database files directly.
The KDC administrator should grant only the
.B l
permission when read-only listing is the intended access.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kadmin (1),
.BR klist (1),
.BR kinit (1),
.BR krb5.conf (5),
.BR kdc.conf (5),
.BR kadm5.acl (5)
